# Malformed IPSec decode corpus (ESP / AH / IKEv2).
#
# Line-oriented, mirroring `malformed/core-decode-corpus.hex`:
#
#   name | target | classification | context-or-field | hex
#
# `target` selects how the raw IPSec bytes in `hex` are reached through a
# *public* decode entrypoint (`resilience.rs` wraps them in the documented
# envelope before decoding):
#
#   ipv4-esp  -> Ipv4(protocol 50) / <hex>, decoded via decode_from_l3(Ipv4)
#   ipv6-esp  -> Ipv6(next-header 50) / <hex>, decoded via decode_from_l3(Ipv6)
#   ipv4-ah   -> Ipv4(protocol 51) / <hex>, decoded via decode_from_l3(Ipv4)
#   ipv6-ah   -> Ipv6(next-header 51) / <hex>, decoded via decode_from_l3(Ipv6)
#   ikev2     -> Ipv4 / Udp(500) / <hex>, decoded via decode_from_l3(Ipv4)
#
# `classification` is the decode contract the crate guarantees for the case:
#
#   structured-error  -> truncation is detectable from the buffer alone, so the
#                        decode MUST return a structured CrafterError (never a
#                        panic). `context-or-field` is the exact BufferTooShort
#                        `context` or InvalidFieldValue `field`.
#   lenient-no-panic  -> the input is "malformed" only in a sense the *opaque*
#                        (no-SA) decode cannot detect (e.g. a bad ESP pad length
#                        living inside the still-encrypted body, or extra
#                        trailing ciphertext). The decode is allowed to succeed
#                        opaquely OR error, but it MUST NOT panic.
#                        `context-or-field` is a free-form note.
#
# All SPIs/keys are documentation-only placeholder bytes; no real key material.

# --- ESP: truncated below the 8-octet SPI||Seq fixed header (RFC 4303 §2) ---
# Detectable truncation -> the opaque ESP decode rejects it with `esp header`.
esp-truncated-header-1 | ipv4-esp | structured-error | esp header | 11
esp-truncated-header-5 | ipv4-esp | structured-error | esp header | 1122334455
esp-truncated-header-7 | ipv4-esp | structured-error | esp header | 11223344556677
esp-truncated-header-7-v6 | ipv6-esp | structured-error | esp header | aabbccdd000000

# --- ESP: full 8-octet header present, opaque body (no SA) ---
# The opaque decode keeps SPI||Seq and preserves the remaining (encrypted) body
# verbatim. A bad ESP pad length and oversized trailing ICV both live *inside*
# the encrypted body, so the no-SA path cannot see them: it must not panic, and
# decoding opaquely is correct. The SA path that *can* see them is exercised by
# the `esp_decode_with_sa_*` unit/integration tests.
esp-opaque-empty-body | ipv4-esp | lenient-no-panic | opaque spi+seq, empty body | aabbccdd00000001
esp-opaque-bad-pad-length-in-body | ipv4-esp | lenient-no-panic | pad length lives inside encrypted body | aabbccdd00000001ff00000000000000ff
esp-opaque-short-icv-tail | ipv6-esp | lenient-no-panic | icv shorter than algorithm needs (encrypted) | aabbccdd0000000112345678

# --- AH: truncated below the 12-octet fixed header (RFC 4302 §2.2) ---
ah-truncated-header-4 | ipv4-ah | structured-error | ah header | 3b000000
ah-truncated-header-11 | ipv4-ah | structured-error | ah header | 3b00000aaabbccdd000000
ah-truncated-header-8-v6 | ipv6-ah | structured-error | ah header | 3b000000aabbccdd

# --- AH: oversized Payload Length declares an ICV the buffer cannot hold ---
# Payload Len 0x0a -> header (0x0a+2)*4 = 48 octets, ICV 36 octets, but only the
# 12 fixed octets are present, so the declared ICV runs off the end -> `ah icv`.
ah-oversized-payload-len | ipv4-ah | structured-error | ah icv | 3b0a0000aabbccdd00000001
ah-oversized-payload-len-v6 | ipv6-ah | structured-error | ah icv | 3bff0000aabbccdd00000001

# --- IKEv2: truncated below the 28-octet header (RFC 7296 §3.1) ---
# The IKE decoder is reached through the UDP/500 application dispatch, which only
# fires for a non-empty UDP payload, so the smallest reachable truncation is a
# 1-octet buffer; a 27-octet buffer is one short of the fixed header.
ike-truncated-header-1 | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.header | 11
ike-truncated-header-27 | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.header | 111111111111111122222222222222220020220800000000000000

# --- IKEv2: header Length field shorter than the 28-octet fixed header ---
# Length = 0x0000000a (10) < 28 -> `ikev2.header.length`.
ike-length-below-header | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.header.length | 1111111111111111222222222222222200202208000000000000000a

# --- IKEv2: header Length claims more bytes than the buffer holds ---
# Length = 0x000000ff (255) but only 28 octets present -> `ikev2.message`.
ike-length-exceeds-buffer | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.message | 111111111111111122222222222222220020220800000000000000ff

# --- IKEv2: Next Payload chain runs off the end of the message ---
# NextPayload 33 (SA) with Length 0x0000001e (30): two trailing octets cannot
# even hold a 4-octet generic payload header -> `ikev2.payload.header`.
ike-next-payload-header-off-end | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.payload.header | 1111111111111111222222222222222221202208000000000000001e0000

# NextPayload 33 (SA) with Length 0x00000020 (32): the 4-octet generic header is
# present but declares its own Length 0x00ff (255), running past the buffer ->
# `ikev2.payload.length`.
ike-next-payload-length-off-end | ikev2 | structured-error | ikev2.payload.length | 11111111111111112222222222222222212022080000000000000020000000ff
